<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Queen Charlotte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charlotte of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> (1744–1818) was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> and queen consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Ireland from 1761. A princess of the small ducal house of Mecklenburg-Strelitz, she bore fifteen children, including the future George IV and William IV. A patron of music and botany, she gave her name to Charlottesville, Charlotte in North Carolina and other places.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlotte-of-mecklenburg-strelitz.jpg" alt="Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Gainsborough · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlotte_gainsborough.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
